## 1. What are generics?

Generics let you **parameterize types**.  
Instead of writing a class, interface, or method that works only with `Object` (or a single concrete type), you write it once and let the compiler enforce that the same type is used everywhere inside it.

> **Why bother?**  
> * **Type safety** – the compiler checks that you never mix up the intended type, so you don’t get a `ClassCastException` at runtime.  
> * **Code reuse** – you write the logic once and use it for `Integer`, `String`, your own `User` class, etc.  
> * **Readability** – `List<String>` instantly tells you *what* the list holds.

Generics were introduced in Java 5, and they work through **type erasure**: the generic type information is removed at compile time and replaced with its bounds (usually `Object`). The JVM never sees `T`; it just sees the underlying type. That’s why you can’t do `new T[10]`, for example.

---

## 2. Basic syntax

```java
// 1. Generic class
class Box<T> {          // <T> declares a type parameter named T
    private T content;

    public Box(T content) { this.content = content; }
    public T get() { return content; }
    public void set(T content) { this.content = content; }
}

// 2. Generic method
class Utils {
    // T is inferred from the arguments
    public static <T> void printAll(T[] array) {
        for (T elem : array) System.out.println(elem);
    }
}
```

* `T` is a **placeholder** that will be replaced by a concrete type when the class/method is used.  
* You can use more than one type parameter: `class Pair<K, V> { … }`.

---

## 3. A simple end‑to‑end example

Let’s build a generic `Box` that can hold any type, and then use it with a few concrete types.

```java
// Generic class
class Box<T> {
    private T content;

    public Box(T content) { this.content = content; }

    public T get() { return content; }

    public void set(T content) { this.content = content; }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return "Box{" + "content=" + content + '}';
    }
}
```

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // 1. Box of Integer
        Box<Integer> intBox = new Box<>(42);
        System.out.println(intBox);          // Box{content=42}
        intBox.set(100);
        System.out.println(intBox.get());    // 100

        // 2. Box of String
        Box<String> strBox = new Box<>("Hello");
        System.out.println(strBox);          // Box{content=Hello}

        // 3. Box of a custom type
        Box<Person> personBox = new Box<>(new Person("Alice", 30));
        System.out.println(personBox);       // Box{content=Person{name='Alice', age=30}}
    }
}

class Person {
    private String name;
    private int age;

    Person(String name, int age) { this.name = name; this.age = age; }

    @Override
    public String toString() { return "Person{name='" + name + "', age=" + age + '}'; }
}
```

### What happened?

| Step | What the compiler checks | Result at runtime |
|------|--------------------------|-------------------|
| `Box<Integer>` | `content` must be an `Integer` (or subtype). | No cast needed. |
| `intBox.set(100)` | `100` is an `int`, autoboxed to `Integer`. | Stored as `Integer`. |
| `Box<String> strBox = new Box<>(42);` | ❌ Compile‑time error: `Integer` is not a `String`. | No code generated. |

---

## 4. A generic method that works with any Comparable type

Sometimes you want a method that can work on *any* type that satisfies a constraint. You use **type bounds**.

```java
public static <T extends Comparable<T>> T max(T a, T b) {
    return (a.compareTo(b) > 0) ? a : b;
}
```

Usage:

```java
System.out.println(max(10, 20));          // 20 (Integer)
System.out.println(max("apple", "banana")); // banana (String)
System.out.println(max(3.14, 2.71));       // 3.14 (Double)
```

* The compiler ensures `T` implements `Comparable<T>`.  
* You cannot pass a type that doesn’t implement that interface.

---

## 5. Wildcards – a quick peek

When you’re writing APIs you sometimes need **covariance** (allowing a super‑type) or **contravariance** (requiring a sub‑type). Wildcards make this possible.

```java
// A method that can read from any List<? extends Number>
static void printNumbers(List<? extends Number> list) {
    for (Number n : list) System.out.println(n);
}

// A method that can accept a List<? super Integer>
static void addIntegers(List<? super Integer> list) {
    list.add(1);
    list.add(2);
}
```

> * `? extends Number` means “any type that is a Number or a subtype.”  
> * `? super Integer` means “any type that is Integer or a super‑type (Object, Number).”

---

## 6. Quick recap

| Feature | What it solves | Example |
|---------|----------------|---------|
| **Generic classes / interfaces** | Reuse code with any type | `Box<T>` |
| **Generic methods** | Operations that need a type parameter | `max(T a, T b)` |
| **Type bounds** | Constrain the allowed types | `<T extends Comparable<T>>` |
| **Wildcards** | Flexibility in APIs (covariance/contravariance) | `List<? extends Number>` |
| **Type erasure** | Runtime representation is the raw type | No `ClassCastException` on `Box<Integer>` |

---

### Final thought

Generics give you *compile‑time type safety* without sacrificing flexibility. Think of them as a way to tell the compiler: “this class/method works with *any* type, but I’ll know which one when I actually use it.” The compiler then checks that you always use the same type consistently. That’s why Java’s collections (`List<String>`, `Map<Integer, Person>`) are so safe and convenient. Happy coding!
